{
  "version": 1,
  "questionId": "q10",
  "speechText": "Yes, I have hands-on experience with tool calling, especially on the backend control side. For example, in Clincove, the model can use read-only tools like search and document read. Then I would expose a small set of backend tools. The model can decide which tool it needs. But the backend still defines the tool contract, validates the arguments, checks permission, executes the action, and returns a structured result. For the interface, we design the tool interface in a familiar way for the LLM, like a file system with search and read, because this usually helps the model perform better. For tracking, I also worked on tool safety, such as audit logs, tracing, citations, and final-response validation. So overall, I directly worked on the Clincove tool-calling flow, and I also understand and can modify parts of the StrangeLoop engine when needed.",
